WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:04.520
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.759
Do you remember the amazing pix2pix algorithm
from last year?

00:00:08.759 --> 00:00:14.610
It was able to perform image translation,
which means that it could take a daytime image

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:22.109
and translate it into a nighttime image, create
maps from satellite images, or create photorealistic

00:00:22.109 --> 00:00:24.460
shoes from a crude drawing.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.769
I remember that I almost fell off the chair
when I've first seen the results.

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:34.580
But this new algorithm takes it up a notch,
and transforms these edge maps into human

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:39.610
faces, not only that, but it also animates
them in time.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.890
As you see here, it also takes into consideration
the fact that the same edges may result in

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:50.410
many different faces, and therefore it is
also willing to gives us more of these options.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:55.330
If I fell out of the chair for the still image
version, I don't really know what the appropriate

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:57.250
reaction would be to this.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:03.800
It can also take a crude map of labels, where
each color corresponds to one object class,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.960
such as roads, cars or buildings, and it follows
how our labels evolve in time and creates

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:11.830
an animation out of it.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:16.641
We can also change the meaning of our labels
easily, for instance, in the lower left, you

00:01:16.641 --> 00:01:21.250
see how the buildings are now suddenly transformed
to trees.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:26.060
Or, we can also change the trees to become
buildings.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:32.840
Do you remember motion transfer from a couple
videos ago?

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:39.130
It can do a similar variant of that too, and
even synthesizes the shadows around the character

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.130
in a reasonably correct manner.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:45.950
As you see, the temporal coherence of this
technique is second to none, which means that

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:50.980
it remembers what it did with past images
and doesn't do anything drastically different

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.190
for the next frame, and therefore generates
smoother videos.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:59.460
This is very apparent, especially when juxtaposed
with the previous pix2pix method.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.260
So, how is this achieved?

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.440
There are three key differences from the previous
technique to achieve this:

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.619
One, the original architecture uses a generator
neural network to create images, where there

00:02:10.619 --> 00:02:17.090
is also a separate discriminator network that
judges its work and teaches it to do better.

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:22.650
Instead, this work uses two discriminator
neural networks, one checks whether the images

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:29.030
look good one by one, and one more discriminator
for overlooking whether the sequence of these

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.720
images would pass as a video.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:37.060
This discriminator cracks down on the generator
network if it creates sequences that are not

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:42.370
temporally coherent and this is why we have
minimal flickering in the output videos.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:43.950
Fantastic idea.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:49.260
Two, to ease the training process, it also
does it progressively, which means that the

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:54.940
network is first faced with an easier version
of the problem that progressively gets harder

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:56.100
over time.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.280
If you have a look at the paper, you will
see that the training is both progressive

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.540
in terms of space and time.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.860
I love this idea too.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:10.810
Three, it also uses a flow map that describes
the changes that took place since the previous

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:11.810
frame.

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:18.040
Note that this pix2pix algorithm was published
in 2017, a little more than a year ago.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:23.440
I think that is a good taste of the pace of
progress in machine learning research.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.230
Up to 2k resolution, 30 seconds of video,
and the source code is available.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:32.530
Congratulations folks, this paper is something
else.

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:35.819
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

